\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Conditional shift in the dataset causes the relative performance gain of our CxVAE over the GVAE.} We show performance on datasets generated with different values of the $\lambda $ hyper-parameter, controlling the amount of conditional shift. For low values of $\lambda $ the conditional distribution of student aptitude given a score changed very little from one group to another, and so the CxVAE and GVAE perform equally well. However, as $\lambda $ increases, the GVAE fails to disentangle.\relax }}{9}{figure.caption.12}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:xy}{{4}{9}{\textbf {Conditional shift in the dataset causes the relative performance gain of our CxVAE over the GVAE.} We show performance on datasets generated with different values of the $\lambda $ hyper-parameter, controlling the amount of conditional shift. For low values of $\lambda $ the conditional distribution of student aptitude given a score changed very little from one group to another, and so the CxVAE and GVAE perform equally well. However, as $\lambda $ increases, the GVAE fails to disentangle.\relax }{figure.caption.12}{}}
